<subsubsection xml:id="sec-all-ManagingAnxiety">
  <title>Managing Anxiety</title>

  <p>It is incredibly important to reduce your overall anxiety because not doing so will ultimately increase it. While you are doing so, though, it is almost guaranteed that you will experience anxiety when you encounter your triggers.</p>

  <p>There are many research-backed methods for dealing with anxiety in those moments. Please keep in mind that these strategies are typically most effective when practiced. All of these strategies can be done during or right before exams or encountering another anxiety-inducing situation.</p>

  <p>Strategies include <url href="https://youtu.be/z7zOjEsyMRE?si=IenGyTO4KDEXHBcp">reflective writing</url>, <url href="https://www.healthline.com/health/breathing-exercises-for-anxiety#alternate-nostrils">breathing exercises</url>, <url href="https://midlandedu-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/g/personal/jkneisley_midland_edu/EYX1hJw9sR5LjXGkTcl3uLUBO21wiyLgk0vQ4ys7yJ0fdQ?e=knd3Ms">coloring mandalas</url> (<url href="https://thewellnesssociety.org/mandala-coloring-sheets/">free coloring pages for printing</url>), <url href="https://youtu.be/SNqYG95j_UQ?si=LDsYJ4yAWveiWrNA">progressive muscle relaxation</url>, and <url href="https://boomboomnaturals.com/blogs/news/how-to-use-mental-anchoring-to-reduce-anxiety?srsltid=AfmBOoruqIVnMHMH_Qg-SchNZ9gHggBT6_2uXp8d_txIWGOllRc4mBQx">anchoring</url>.</p>
</subsubsection>
